{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short\ncat collab/1792350000000-review-6c-layouts.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.report.md collab/1792370000000-engineer-6c-fix1.report.md\ncat docs/agents/build-n-test.md\n git diff --stat 47ddb1d9..HEAD'","aggregated_output":"?? collab/1792340000000-engineer-6c-layouts.opus.stdout.log\n?? collab/1792340000000-engineer-6c-layouts.prompt.md\n?? collab/1792340000000-engineer-6c-layouts.report.md\n?? collab/1792350000000-review-6c-layouts.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1792350000000-review-6c-layouts.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1792350000000-review-6c-layouts.prompt.md\n?? collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.fable.stdout.log\n?? collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.prompt.md\n?? collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.report.md\n?? collab/1792370000000-engineer-6c-fix1.opus.stdout.log\n?? collab/1792370000000-engineer-6c-fix1.prompt.md\n?? collab/1792370000000-engineer-6c-fix1.report.md\n?? collab/1792380000000-review-6c-fix1.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1792380000000-review-6c-fix1.prompt.md\n# Slice 6c layout review — gpt-6.1-sol\n\nReviewed branch `srvm-phase6c`, HEAD `47ddb1d9`, `git diff master...HEAD`, against the current continuation design §§4, 5.5, 5.6, 8.3 and the engineer report. This is an independent review with independently applied mutants. No production or test changes are proposed in this worktree; only this findings file remains from the review.\n\n**The JVM suites pass, but the slice is not ready to merge.** Free-environment values bypass conversion, legitimate alpha-distinct source segments lose their identity at native-image lookup, the acceptance matrix is reduced without an approved design amendment, and law 4 lacks decisive negative controls.\n\n## Findings\n\n| ID | Priority | Location | Finding and required action |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | P1 | `layout.cljc:455, 562, 729-730, 790` | Free-environment values are copied unchanged in both directions. `canonical-env` and `canonical-closure` also merge the raw kernel free environment. A carried canonical closure in `free-env` remains `{:params … :entry … :segment … :env …}` in the receiving de Bruijn kernel, which expects `:arity/:body-pc/:frames`; both receivers attach successfully and then throw on invocation. Convert the task's free-environment values and use the converted environment consistently for E reconstruction, including nested collections/closures/continuations. Add a real cross-engine case with a callable closure supplied through `free-env`. |\n| F2 | P1 | `layout.cljc:177-184, 298-302, 751-769`; harness `kernel-space:124` | `:ambiguous-image` rejects legitimate programs. `(fn [x] x)` and `(fn [y] y)` have different A but identical H **and** R. Attaching both and halting with the second closure makes `canonical-task` refuse `:ambiguous-image`. Lowering can similarly attach two A to one deduplicated native image and leave a state that cannot relift. Retain A provenance for activations, return frames and closures through an explicit kernel/composition seam; preflight collisions before stream attachment until that seam exists. Never choose an arbitrary A. |\n| F3 | P1 | harness `link-rows:1390`, `unlowerable:1532`; design §8.3(9–11) | The required matrix is incomplete: rows 7–12 and 17 are excluded on both native lanes; row 4 loses four transfers. Full law 4 covers 61 rather than 89 cases; law 5 covers 350 rather than 534 ordered transfers. Tail acceptance covers rows 6 and 20, omitting 9 and 12. Exclusions are disclosed honestly, but disclosure is not acceptance. Land the retained-FFI seam and resolve the native-link/install scope with the Architect; restore the matrix or obtain an explicit scope amendment. |\n| F4 | P2 | harness `canonical-equality:1490-1499`, `logical-positions:1451-1465`; `rows.cljc:957-978` | Law 4 passes with only the reference lane compared; it also passes when every cursor position is replaced by `:constant`. Both survive quick and slow tests. Pin exactly three distinct engine lanes and exercise negative controls that change cursor position, stream association, lexical values and saved values. Require injective stream correspondences and check descriptor equivalence separately: current `canonical-under` erases the entire descriptor, including an independently added nonidentity field. |\n| F5 | P2 | `layout.cljc:224-237`; `layout_test.cljc:54-79`; harness `layout-refusals:1628` | The mutant selecting the first of ambiguous preimages survives quick and slow tests. Existing inverse tests cover only allocator-guaranteed live uniqueness; the upward-tamper fixture deliberately chooses a unique dead preimage. Add a negative test for an ambiguous dead-slot preimage, and pin the `:register-layout` refusal. Adopt an explicit design rule for the unique-preimage extension. |\n| F6 | P3 | `layout_test.cljc:13` | Kondo reports `yin.vm.debruijn.register` required but never used. Remove the unused `rvm` require. Acceptance 13's lint requirement currently fails. |\n\n## Verification and concrete probes\n\nCommands run sequentially in foreground processes:\n\n```sh\nclojure -M:test -e :slow -n yin.vm.semantic-register.layout-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test\nclojure -M:test -i :slow -n yin.vm.semantic-register.layout-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test\nclojure -M:kondo --lint src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc test/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout_test.cljc test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc\n```\n\n- Quick: **60 tests, 3,615 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n- Slow: **4 tests, 2,419 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n- Kondo: **0 errors, 1 warning**, exit 2; the unused require above. Standalone `clj-kondo` is not installed, but the repository alias works. The engineer's permission blockage does not prevent lint verification in this review.\n- Node and Dart were not executed. This review's requested JVM commands do not discharge §8.3(13)'s three-host gate.\n\nAdditional probes ran against restored, unmodified code using a temporary Clojure script, outside the repository:\n\n1. **Alpha ambiguity:** project `(fn [x] x)` and `(fn [y] y)`; verify different `:address`, identical `:stack-id` and identical `:register-id`; attach both on the stack lane, run the latter to a closure, call `canonical-task` with both segments. The kernel halts normally; lift returns `:yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image`. This is a legitimate program rejected by the current image-only provenance. The harness's global `code-index` can also include an alpha-equivalent A from an unrelated earlier fixture, because `kernel-space` selects by image checksum, further widening false refusals.\n2. **Free closure:** create a reference-lane identity closure `(fn [arg] arg)`, put it in initial `free-env` as `f`, put an authentic host cursor in `free-env` as `c`, and park `(then (next-of c) (f \"B\"))`. Lift and lower to each native lane. Each lower attaches one stream and leaves `f`'s payload in canonical form (`:params [arg], :entry 2, :segment -1, :env {}`), rather than positional form. The original reference machine returns `\"B\"`. Feeding the read and invoking `f` throws a NullPointerException in both native receivers. This is independent of primitive refusal D4.\n3. **Retained FFI:** call `crossed` directly for row 4, bypassing `unlowerable`. All four distinct pairs with a native receiver remain `{:halted false :value :yin/blocked}` after the expected request/response sequence. D2 is reproduced, not merely inferred.\n4. **Normalizer controls:** changing cursor position data changes the baseline `logical-root`; adding `:review/extra :different` to each stream descriptor does **not** change it. The present position normalizer retains nonidentity data, but the suite does not protect that property; descriptor erasure is already part of `canonical-under`'s specified behavior and must be guarded by a separate equivalence assertion or revised normalization contract.\n\n## Independent mutants\n\nEach mutant changed exactly one backed-up source/test file, ran the two layout/harness namespaces in the quick tier, and was restored before the next mutant. Backups were made with `shutil.copy2` (the equivalent of `cp`), then checked with `diff -q` and `git diff --quiet -- <file>` after every restoration, including the final restoration. All three quick survivors were independently rerun against the slow tier of the same namespaces. Temporary scripts, backups and logs are under `/tmp`, not repository changes.\n\n| Mutant | Actual change | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| M1 | Top and K register `rd` from physical `entry.dest` / `f.dest` | Killed: `register-rd-from-a`; law 4/5 errors; refusal checks. 6 failures, 2 errors. |\n| M2 | `register-live` no longer subtracts virtual `rd` | Killed: `register-live-is-live-out`; 56 failures. |\n| M3 | Lowered stack base is cumulative height **before** the current return slice | Killed: pinned multi-frame bases and subsequent continuation; 1 failure, 1 error. Quick bare transfers alone would not expose the nonempty-slice error. |\n| M4 | Tail `live-out` incorrectly falls through to `p+1` | Killed: the added `:tail-operand` fixture; 2 failures. |\n| M5 | Merge lexical binding under the accumulated outer/free environment | Killed: environment reconstruction and canonical equality; 41 failures. |\n| M6 | Canonical closure drops its last binder | Killed: closure round trip and cross-engine tests; 15 failures, 3 errors. |\n| M7 | `cross-lowering` iterates `(rest ordered-pairs)` | Killed: explicit pair counts; 16 failures. |\n| M8 | `rows/lower-task` executes before `lower-defect` | Killed: `layout-refusals` attachment count; 1 failure. |\n| M9 | Ambiguous `virt` returns `(first live)` or `(first named)` instead of refusing | **SURVIVES quick and slow.** Quick: 31 tests / 3,162 assertions; slow: 3 tests / 2,411 assertions. |\n| M10 | Each K slice ends at `min(n, hi+1)`, overlapping the next slice by one | Killed: partition/length checks and dynamic lifts; 131 failures, 56 errors. |\n| M11 | Law 4 builds roots using `(take 1 lanes)` | **SURVIVES quick and slow.** Quick still runs 31 tests, now 3,068 assertions; slow: 3 tests / 2,053 assertions. |\n| M12 | `logical-positions` replaces every cursor position with `:constant` | **SURVIVES quick and slow.** Quick: 31 tests / 3,162 assertions; slow: 3 tests / 2,411 assertions. This erases offsets and identity association, not merely one identity spelling. |\n\nThe surviving mutants are test defects, not evidence that baseline `virt` or `logical-positions` currently makes those substitutions. Baseline `virt` refuses ambiguity. Baseline `logical-positions` changes only recognized ringbuffer identity fields and keeps numeric offsets.\n\n## Static and task-level assessment\n\n**Register pairing and live sets:** `register-pairs` aligns destination/uses with opcode operand order; `phys-maps` is body-scoped and first-wins, with the corpus testing every pair for agreement. At reachable boundary pcs the inverse over actual successor liveness is checked, with subtraction of virtual `rd` before mapping. Tail boundaries have no successors and `[]`. Top and each K frame recover `rd` independently from A, not the physical destination. Baseline implementation is correct on these checks. The unique-dead-preimage fallback is defensible for diagnosing tampered windows, provided its domain and ambiguity refusal become normative and receive negative tests.\n\n**Stack:** non-push H/A correspondence, ancestor operand ordering, saved-set equality at non-tail parking instructions, `[previous-base, base)` slicing and cumulative-including lowering agree with current §4.3. The fixture pins `[0 0 0 2 4]`, six operands and five K frames. The before-slice rule is wrong and independently killed. Tail lift discards the current window/E; lower fills `[]`/nil, and both tested tail rows continue identically with junk fills.\n\n**Environments and closures:** chain order, rightmost-wins duplicate params, nil-fill and shadowing agree with §4.4. The positional `[1 2] → canonical → [2 2]` duplicate-binder outcome is permitted: the law is canonical equality, not hidden positional-state equality. Closure arity/binders/entry and captured lexical frames are reconstructed from A and tested. **F1 limits this positive result:** the task's free environment is raw in the conversion contexts, so closures or captures inside that environment do not share the same value conversion as stores, windows and lexical frames.\n\n**Tasks and refusals:** `canonical-task` checks active control, refuses install children, carries resources/counters/waits/parked records and feeds the ordinary row carrier. `kernel-task` derives/attaches A's images and converts stores, parked records and waits. `lower-task` runs `lower-defect` before `rows/lower-task`, therefore before any stream attachment; M8 proves the current refusal test detects reversal. Install-child lower refusal is present, but there is no dedicated before-attach install refusal assertion in the new test. `:continuation-registers` scans primitives through wait/parked state windows, lexical bindings, guest collections, closure lexical captures and reified states. It intentionally excludes the free part of E because the kernel's free environment can hold primitives. This is a state-payload restriction, not a ban on all `:prim` rows.\n\n**Law 4/5 honesty:** baseline `machine` genuinely dispatches to three kernels, and `load-program` derives H/R for native lanes. It does not simply feed all lanes the reference VM. `crossed` explicitly asserts the receiving engine, and law 5's counts kill a skipped pair. In law 4, `apply =` over a singleton returns true; fixture-case counts do not establish lane coverage. Logical stream names come from resource keys rather than host traversal order; this is reasonable for these fixtures, but correspondence injectivity and stream/descriptor/position coherence need explicit controls. The broad descriptor erasure is design-sanctioned and cannot establish equality of arbitrary carrier states.\n\n## Deviations and D1–D5: Architect rulings and exact amendments\n\nAPI names and the scratch semantic-register restore composition (deviations 1 and 2) are acceptable implementation choices: the required algorithms exist and reuse carrier validation. The reduced corpus avoiding ANTLR, safepoint-producing variants, and visible duplicate-binder round trips (5 and 6) are reasonable and disclosed. The fallback and primitive restriction (7 and 8) need the normative wording below. Exclusions (3 and 4) describe real limitations but do not fulfill the current acceptance contract.\n\n**D1 — ACCEPT WITH CHANGES.** Ringbuffer positions contain identity; the design's claim that marker substitution removes every lane identity is false. The current narrowly targeted rewrite is reasonable, but its protection against erasing state is untested. Replace §5.5's claim that all remaining procedure inputs are identity-free with:\n\n> A cursor position may contain a stream identity. The fixture supplies an explicit, injective logical-stream correspondence and a transport-specific position renaming operation. That operation replaces only identity-bearing fields, preserves every nonidentity field including offsets, and rejects missing coverage or disagreement with the cursor body's stream. Stream descriptors must be shown equivalent under the same correspondence before comparison; replacing a whole descriptor with a logical name alone does not prove equivalence. After these checks, canonical-under re-emits and renumbers the task. The harness includes negative controls proving that a changed position offset, descriptor nonidentity field, saved value or lexical value changes the compared state, and asserts exactly the three distinct kernel lanes for every law-4 case.\n\n**D2 — ACCEPT; MUST RESOLVE THE GATE.** The kernel re-park selects its own response pair. Insert in §4's required native-kernel seams:\n\n> On completion of a retained FFI request, each de Bruijn kernel preserves the carried response cursor and response stream when constructing the response wait. If both are present, response-wait-entry is overridden with cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref :id response-cursor} and stream-id response-stream. A partial carried pair is rejected; the kernel's composition-owned response pair is used only for entries that carry neither field. Response decoding precedes delivery, including tail delivery. Row 4 must continue on all six ordered engine pairs with the receiving composition's call pair distinct from the source's pair. This seam is a prerequisite of 6c acceptance, like the stack tail-completion seam.\n\nThe non-editable-kernel scope makes this a prerequisite/Architect decision, not a reason to silently count four missing transfers as passing. For link/install rows, the Architect must separately provide A-to-native module response adaptation and recursive install-task layouts or explicitly narrow §§5.1/5.4/5.6(8)/8.3(9–11). The current gate remains incomplete pending that decision.\n\n**D3 — ACCEPT WITH CHANGES.** Keep the restricted inverse for valid states, but define diagnostics outside its domain. Append to §4.2:\n\n> At a reachable boundary, virt first considers preimages in live-out*. Exactly one is returned. If there are none, it may return the body's sole named virtual preimage, solely to reconstruct a tampered window for validation. Zero or multiple such preimages, or multiple live preimages, are refused as :yin.k/non-portable :register-layout; no enumeration order selects a preimage. The harness checks an ambiguous dead physical slot is refused, a unique dead slot yields :window-extra through the frame validator, and a missing live slot yields :window-missing.\n\nAn ambiguous tamper cannot honestly be promised the `:window-extra` kind; amend §8.3(12) to permit this explicit layout refusal in that case.\n\n**D4 — ACCEPT WITH CHANGES.** Primitives are legal carrier values; the limitation belongs to the receiving native kernel. Replace §8.3(6)'s phrase “a primitive … is no carrier value” and add to §§4.2/7.2:\n\n> A primitive function is an admitted carrier value (:prim). The current de Bruijn register kernel cannot restore a primitive function reachable through its saved register or lexical-frame payload, including nested guest collections, closure captures and reified continuation state. Lowering a task with such a wait or parked-state payload into that kernel refuses :yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers, with the payload path, before stream attachment. This restriction does not prohibit primitives in the task-wide free environment or in carrier values a kernel can otherwise hold. Cross-engine acceptance explicitly records unsupported payload cases; expanding this receiver domain requires a separate kernel seam.\n\nThe present negative case demonstrates a primitive in a window. Add distinct lexical/captured/nested-value refusal fixtures; do not equate those untested paths with full mutation coverage.\n\n**D5 — ACCEPT.** Row 21 captures the innermost wrapper's `x`, not a symbol insulated from the wrapper. Add to §5.2 row 21's expectation:\n\n> Under :depth-3, the closure captures the innermost wrapper binding x = 1; the task's free-env retains x = 99. The blocked read's E therefore binds x to 1, and all three lanes agree on that lexical result. The bare row tests the free binding 99. This wrapper-induced lexical capture is intentional and is pinned separately from free-environment preservation.\n\nNo production patch is needed for D5; the existing `env-reconstruction` test already pins read E = 1 and free-env = 99.\n\n**Additional necessary identity amendment (F2), §4.1/6.1:**\n\n> A is the code identity even when distinct A derive identical H or R. Native-image hashes remain checksums and must not select a source segment. The composition retains the A provenance of every current activation, return frame and closure through native attach and restore, permitting several A to share an image without conflation. Until the requisite provenance seam exists, a receiver preflights image collisions before stream attachment and reports the unsupported provenance case; this temporary refusal does not satisfy the full canonical round-trip gate for legitimate multi-A programs.\n\n## Portability\n\nStatic review found no newly introduced `for` over long row sequences, private-var test access, multi-key `assoc` starting from nil, or unary-minus float use. Maps with multiple keys start from `{}` or known maps; pc arithmetic uses integer additions/subtraction. Catch branches include `:cljd Object` first. `satisfies?` is used for protocol dispatch and descriptor checks; `binding` wraps synchronous fixture construction; `memoize` caches the pure static segment derivation.\n\nThe **pinned** ClojureDart source (`sonwh98/clojuredart`, SHA `7d00ee2aa7fa9d990581e78ebc4a66b93cb70a31`) implements `satisfies?` (`core.cljd:513`), `memoize` (`:6809`) and `binding` (`:7623`, using Dart zones). Thus their mere presence is not a demonstrated Dart incompatibility. These are also ordinary supported ClojureScript forms. This source check is not a Node/Dart compilation or runtime result. Acceptance 13 and the phase-7 entry gate still require their prescribed host verification.\n\nFinal tracked-source restoration was checked with backup `diff` and `git diff --quiet`. Existing untracked engineer/review prompts and stdout logs were left alone.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 +07\n# Architect rulings for slice 6c (claude-fable-5-1)\n\nRead: codex's review (F1-F6, probes, D1-D5), opus's report, the continuation design §§0.1, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8.3, 11, `layout.cljc` (HEAD 47ddb1d9), the harness lanes, the frame validator's E2, the carrier's `restore-task`, both de Bruijn kernels' `:request-sent` arms and `attach-image`, the semantic-register kernel's `admit-image` and `register-restore`, and `store_write_audit_test.clj`. I ran the audit test once (it fails on exactly the two layout sites the brief names). No doc, source or test was edited.\n\n## Summary (12 lines)\n\n1. Merge 6c after a fix slice of about 2.5 days on opus; no kernel is edited, no acceptance item is dropped silently.\n2. R1: alpha-equivalent segments collide only in the de Bruijn kernels, whose `attach-image` dedupes by R/H; the semantic-register kernel keys code by A and has no collision. Refuse the collision before attach, document it as a phase-8-bounded limit, and test that the survivor is unaffected.\n3. R2: make the 61/350 native matrix normative in §5.3 and §8.3; the reference lane keeps all 89/534. This changes acceptance counts, so it is an OWNER decision (Q1). The retained-FFI seam is cheap (half a day, both kernels) if the owner wants row 4 anyway (Q2); link and install on native lanes are not worth 2-3 days of code that dies at phase 8.\n4. R3: a closure or continuation in the task-wide free environment has no finite canonical form on a de Bruijn kernel (its env would contain itself through the shared free environment); refuse it before attach in both directions on the native lanes only. The carrier and the reference lane keep admitting it.\n5. R4: `canonical-under` may erase a stream marker's identity and descriptor; the harness must then prove descriptor equivalence separately, pin three distinct kernel kinds, assert an injective correspondence, and run four negative controls. `logical-positions` is accepted as is.\n6. R5: adopt codex's D3 wording; an ambiguous upward tamper may be refused `:yin.k/non-portable :register-layout` (amend §8.3 item 12).\n7. R6: D1, D3, D4, D5 accepted as codex wrote them; D2 accepted as a seam specification but moved out of 6c's gate (owner Q2); F2's identity amendment accepted with the provenance-seam sentence replaced (text below).\n8. Store audit: allowlist the two sites with the same reason `rows/restore-task` has; do not route them through `store-put`.\n9. What we give up: native-lane coverage of link, install and retained FFI, and closures in the free environment on native lanes, all until phase 8 deletes those kernels.\n10. What we keep: every invariant (A the only identity, tree always available, sharing survives transport, BLAKE3, no kernel edits in phase 6).\n11. Cost: about 2.5 days opus plus a mechanical doc pass and the three-host run; the optional D2 seam adds half a day as its own slice.\n12. READY once the work list is green on three hosts, kondo clean, audit green, and the owner has answered Q1 (Q2 can wait).\n\n## R1 (F2): `:ambiguous-image`\n\n**Facts.** Both de Bruijn `attach-image`s refuse a second attach whose identity is already a row (`register.cljc:211`, the stack kernel likewise). So a native kernel holding `(fn [x] x)` and `(fn [y] y)` physically holds one image under one R or H; its `:images` table cannot name which A an activation came from, and nothing the layout reads can recover it. The semantic-register kernel's `admit-image` keys code by `jing/segment-key v`, which is A (`semantic_register.cljc:297-300, 431`), so two alpha-equivalent vectors are two code entries and the survivor has no collision at all.\n\n**Ruling: (a).** Option (b) is an edit to both kernels' offset tables and both restores, forbidden in phase 6 and deleted at phase 8; not worth a slice. Refuse the collision as early as possible and document it as a limit bounded by phase 8:\n\n- **Lower** into a de Bruijn kernel: `lower-defect` gains a first clause: when two carried A derive one image of the receiver's kind (`:register-id` or `:stack-id` over the carried `code` slot), refuse `:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident :yin.k/segments #{A …}}` before any attach. Status `:unsupported` because it is the receiver's domain, like `:continuation-registers`; the task is a valid carrier.\n- **Lift** from a de Bruijn kernel: the existing `:yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image` stays, but `canonical-task` must raise it before `lift-context` serves anything (it already runs before `rows/lift-task`, which is the only server; assert zero serves in the test).\n- **Never choose an A.** Agreed with codex.\n- **Harness defect to fix alongside:** `kernel-space` filters the global `code-index` by image checksum, so an alpha-equivalent A from an unrelated fixture widens the refusal. Scope it: `project` records into a `binding`-scoped atom `*projected*` when bound, and `kernel-space` uses that set when bound, the global index otherwise. The space is then the vectors this world projected, never the file's history.\n\n**Wording, §4.1 (replacing codex's \"Additional necessary identity amendment\"):**\n\n> A is the code identity even when distinct A derive identical H or R. Native-image hashes remain checksums and must not select a source segment. The de Bruijn kernels' offset tables carry only the image checksum and refuse a second attach of a known checksum, so a de Bruijn kernel cannot hold two segments of one image. Until phase 8 removes those kernels, a de Bruijn receiver refuses a carried task whose code holds two A of one image before any stream is attached (`:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image`), and a de Bruijn source whose space resolves one image to several A refuses the lift (`:yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image`); no enumeration order selects an A. The semantic-register kernel keys code by A and holds alpha-equivalent segments side by side; this limit is the native kernels', not the carrier's.\n\n**Tests.** `layout-test/alpha-equivalent-segments`: project `(fn [x] x)` and `(fn [y] y)`; assert distinct `:address`, equal `:register-id` and equal `:stack-id`; `layout/space` of both, then `image-segment` through `lift-state` refuses `:ambiguous-image` on both lanes with zero serves; lowering a reference-lane task carrying both closures as store values into each native lane refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image` with zero attaches; the same task lowers into the reference lane, relifts and continues (laws 1 and 2 through `handed-off`), which is the survivor check.\n\nCost: 0.5 day.\n\n## R2 (F3): the 89/534 matrix on the native lanes\n\n**Facts.** Link rows need the harness to answer a `require` with the kernel's own format (H or R) while module responses carry A; install children are whole kernels the layout would have to lift and lower recursively, with a link receiver answering natively; the retained-FFI re-park in both kernels names the composition's own pair (`register.cljc:508-514`, `stack.cljc:564-570`), while the semantic-register kernel honours the carried pair in four lines (`semantic_register.cljc:105-111`).\n\n**Ruling: (a), with the counts made normative; the D2 seam offered separately.** Cost of (b) for link and install is 2-3 days of harness and layout code for kernels deleted at phase 8; I do not recommend it. The D2 seam itself is half a day for both kernels (the four-line `cond->` the semantic-register kernel already has, plus a partial-pair refusal in `lower-defect` rather than in the kernel) but it is a kernel edit, so it needs its own slice (6c-post-D2) and an owner yes. This reduces the acceptance counts of §8.3 items 10 and 11 on the native lanes, so **the owner decides (Q1)**; my recommendation is to accept (a).\n\n**Exact counts under (a).** Law 4: 61 cases (15 wrapped rows × 4 segments + row 20), every case comparing exactly three lanes; the reference lane's law 1/2 matrix stays 89. Law 5: 350 ordered transfers (61 × 6 − 4 × 4 for row 4's four transfers into a de Bruijn receiver); if 6c-post-D2 lands, 366 (61 × 6). Bare tier: law 4 16 cases, law 5 92 (96 with the seam). Tail acceptance (§5.6 item 8): rows 6 and 20 on the native lanes; rows 9 and 12 on the reference lane only.\n\n**Wording, §5.3, append:**\n\n> The de Bruijn lanes execute the shared fragment of the matrix: rows 1-6, 13-16, 18-19, 21-22 and 24 under the four wrappers, and row 20. Rows 7-12 and 17 are not built on them: a de Bruijn kernel links a native image while module responses carry A, and §4 defines no layout for install children. Row 4 is lifted from a de Bruijn source but not lowered into a de Bruijn receiver until the retained-FFI seam (§4, 6c-post-D2) lands: the kernel's re-park names its own call pair. These lanes are a cross-engine check of the canonical frame and are removed with their kernels at phase 8; the reference lane runs the whole matrix.\n\n**Wording, §8.3 items 10 and 11, replace:**\n\n> 10. `harness/cross-engine-canonical-equality`: law 4 for every case of the shared fragment under `canonical-under`: 61 cases, each over exactly three distinct kernel lanes.\n> 11. `harness/cross-engine-lowering`: law 5, six ordered pairs over the shared fragment: 350 transfers (366 once 6c-post-D2 lands, when `unlowerable` is emptied).\n\n**Wording, §4 (new paragraph after the stack tail-completion seam), codex's D2 text accepted verbatim except the last sentence, which becomes:**\n\n> This seam is slice 6c-post-D2, a kernel edit of its own, gated by the owner; it is not a prerequisite of 6c. When it lands, `unlowerable` is emptied and the law-5 counts rise to 366 and 96.\n\n## R3 (F1): free-environment values on the native lanes\n\n**Facts.** `kernel-task` copies `:free-env` raw; `canonical-task` and `lift-context` use the kernel's raw `:free-env`. Codex's probe 2 reproduces the break. Beyond that, the lift direction has no finite answer for a closure *in* the free environment: a de Bruijn closure resolves free names dynamically through the one shared `free-env`, so its canonical `env` is `(merge FE* frames)` where `FE*` contains the closure itself; the canonical closure is cyclic. The semantic-register kernel snapshots `E` at creation, so its closures are finite. E2 checks frame environments only (`frame.cljc:137-143`), never a closure value's `env`, so the carrier admits a closure in the free environment whose env disagrees with the free environment on free names, which a de Bruijn kernel cannot honour.\n\n**Ruling: refuse, do not convert.** Conversion on lower would be sound only under an E2-like agreement condition the validator does not check, and conversion on lift is undefined. The honest and cheapest rule, with a shelf life to phase 8:\n\n- `lower-defect` (both de Bruijn kinds): a `:closure` or `:reified` row value in the carried `free-env` slot refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :free-env-value {:yin.k/path [:free-env sym]}` before any attach. Primitives, data and cursor references stay admitted.\n- `canonical-task` (both de Bruijn kinds): a closure or continuation value in the kernel's `:free-env` refuses `:yin.k/non-portable :free-env-value {:yin.k/key sym}` before anything is served.\n- Everything else in the free environment is copied as now; the values that remain need no conversion (`walk-value` converts only closures and continuations).\n\n**Wording, §6.3, append:**\n\n> A de Bruijn kernel resolves free names through its single `free-env` while a canonical closure snapshots its `env`; a closure or continuation held in the free environment therefore has no finite canonical form on a de Bruijn kernel. Until phase 8, a de Bruijn receiver refuses such a value in the carried `free-env` before attach (`:yin.k/unsupported :free-env-value`, with the path), and a de Bruijn source refuses it at lift before serving. The carrier and the semantic-register kernel admit it.\n\n**Test.** `layout-test/free-env-values`: codex's probe 2 as a fixture (reference-lane identity closure `f` in the initial env, `(then (next-of c) (f \"B\"))`): reference run answers `\"B\"`; `handed-off` on the reference lane answers `\"B\"` (carrier still admits it); `lane-lower` into `:stack` and `:register` refuses `[:yin.k/unsupported :free-env-value]` with zero attaches and the path `[:free-env f]`. Lift side: a native kernel whose `:free-env` holds a `lane-closure` refuses at `canonical-task` with zero serves, on both lanes.\n\nCost: 0.5 day.\n\n## R4 (F4): law 4 negative controls and what `canonical-under` erases\n\n**Ruling on D1: accept codex's §5.5 replacement verbatim.** `canonical-under` may erase exactly one thing: the stream marker's identity and descriptor, replaced by the logical name (`rows.cljc:971-974`). That erasure is design-sanctioned and stays; what it cannot prove, descriptor equivalence, the harness proves separately. `logical-positions` is accepted as written: it rewrites only `:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity` inside a cursor-cell body and keeps every other field, which is exactly the \"replaces only identity-bearing fields, preserves every nonidentity field including offsets\" rule.\n\n**Controls the harness must add (one deftest, quick tier, on row 1 bare):**\n\n1. **Three distinct lanes.** `canonical-equality` asserts `(= [:semantic-register :stack :register] (mapv (comp lane-of :machine) worlds))` for every case, and `(= 3 (count roots))`. Kills M11.\n2. **Injective correspondence.** `logical-names` and `ring-names` assert `(= (count m) (count (distinct (vals m))))` and that `logical-positions` renamed every ringbuffer identity (none of the lane identities survives in the emitted rows).\n3. **Descriptor equivalence.** For each logical name, the three lanes' `stream/descriptor` with identity fields renamed under the correspondence are equal; a descriptor given `:review/extra :different` on one lane fails this assertion (codex's probe 4).\n4. **Position control.** A cursor position's offset changed by one on one lane changes the compared root (`logical-root`). Kills M12.\n5. **Value controls.** A saved window value and a lexical `E` value changed on one lane each change the root.\n\nCost: 0.5 day. No doc change beyond D1.\n\n## R5 (F5, D3): the unique-preimage rule for `virt`\n\n**Ruling: adopt codex's D3 wording verbatim** as an appendix to §4.2 (the live-out* rule, then the sole-named-preimage fallback only for validation, refusal on zero or several, no enumeration order). The fallback is a diagnostic extension, not a layout claim, and lives only until phase 8.\n\n**§8.3 item 12, replace the second clause with:**\n\n> a register entry whose `live` was tampered reconstructs a frame the validator refuses (`:window-extra` for a unique dead slot, `:window-missing` for a dropped live slot), or is refused by the layout itself as `:yin.k/non-portable :register-layout` when the added slot is an ambiguous dead preimage; the test pins all three.\n\n**Test.** Extend `layout-refusals`: find, over C, a body and a reachable non-tail boundary whose `:phys` map sends two or more virtuals to one slot outside `live-out*` and the destination; tamper `:live`/`:regs` with that slot; `lift-state` refuses `:register-layout` (status `:yin.k/non-portable`, the pc and slot in the data). Assert at least one such fixture exists in C (the allocator reuses slots, so it will). Kills M9.\n\nCost: 0.25 day.\n\n## R6: D1-D5 wording, and the store-write audit\n\n- **D1**: accept codex's text verbatim (§5.5). Controls per R4.\n- **D2**: accept the seam text verbatim as the §4 seam specification; last sentence replaced per R2. Not a 6c gate.\n- **D3**: accept verbatim (§4.2 appendix). §8.3 item 12 per R5.\n- **D4**: accept verbatim (§8.3 item 6 rephrased; §4.2/§7.2 paragraph). Add the three refusal fixtures codex asks for: a primitive in a lexical binding, in a closure's captured frame, and nested in a guest collection inside a window; each refuses `:continuation-registers` with the stated path before attach. 0.25 day.\n- **D5**: accept verbatim (§5.2 row 21). No code.\n- **F2 identity amendment**: codex's text with the provenance-seam sentences replaced by my R1 wording above.\n- **F6**: remove the unused `rvm` require in `layout_test.cljc`.\n\n**Store-write audit.** The two sites, `[\"canonical-task\" \"assoc\"] 1` and `[\"kernel-task\" \"assoc\"] 1`, go on the **allowlist**, not through `store-put`. Reason, to write beside the entry: both are state construction over keys that already passed the one program-write gate. `canonical-task` copies a quiescent kernel's store whose every program write went through `engine/put-active` and so `store-put`; `kernel-task` copies the scratch machine's store that `rows/restore-task` built key by key through `store-put` (`rows.cljc:1803`). The layout converts values only (`conv`) and never introduces a key, so a second key check would prove nothing, and `store-put` is per key, so routing through it would mean a reduce that re-checks what the carrier's step already checked. This is precisely the precedent of `rows.cljc` `[\"restore-task\" \"assoc\"]` and `handoff.cljc` `[\"isolated-receiver\" \"assoc\"]`.\n\n## Work list for opus (priority order, with acceptance)\n\n1. **F6 and the audit** (0.2 d). Drop the unused require; add the two allowlist entries with the reason above. Accept: `clj -M:kondo` 0 warnings on the three files; `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.store-write-audit-test` green.\n2. **R3 free-env refusals** (0.5 d). `lower-defect` and `canonical-task` as ruled. Accept: `layout-test/free-env-values` as specified, zero attaches and zero serves asserted.\n3. **R1 collision preflight and harness scoping** (0.5 d). `lower-defect` first clause; `*projected*` scoping of `kernel-space`. Accept: `layout-test/alpha-equivalent-segments` as specified, including the reference-lane survivor round trip.\n4. **R4 law-4 controls** (0.5 d). The five controls in one deftest; the three-lane and injectivity assertions inside `canonical-equality` and the name maps. Accept: M11 and M12 re-applied are killed; codex's probe 4 descriptor change fails the descriptor assertion.\n5. **R5 ambiguous preimage** (0.25 d). Accept: M9 re-applied is killed; `layout-refusals` pins `:window-extra`, `:window-missing` and `:register-layout`.\n6. **D4 fixtures** (0.25 d). Accept: three new refusals with paths, zero attaches.\n7. **Counts** (0.1 d). Docstrings of `link-rows`, `unlowerable` and the two matrices cite §5.3's new paragraph; counts unchanged (61/350, 16/92) unless Q2 lands first.\n8. **Doc pass** (0.25 d, mechanical, may be a separate claude -p): apply the verbatim texts in R1, R2, R3, R4 (D1), R5 (D3 and item 12), R6 (D2, D4, D5, F2) by section; §11 gains Q1 and Q2 and their answers.\n9. **Three hosts** (acceptance 13). `bb test:sub yin.vm` on JVM, Node, Dart in the foreground; `satisfies?`, `binding` and `memoize` are pinned-source-supported on Dart per codex but unrun. Accept: three green lanes reported with counts.\n\nTotal about 2.5 days plus the three-host run. Re-review by codex on the diff of items 2-6 only.\n\n## Open questions for the OWNER\n\n- **Q1 (scope, changes acceptance counts).** Accept the native-lane matrix as the shared fragment: law 4 61 of 89, law 5 350 of 534, tail acceptance rows 6 and 20 only, with the reason that those kernels die at phase 8 and the reference lane keeps the full matrix. Recommendation: yes.\n- **Q2 (kernel edit, own slice).** Land 6c-post-D2, the four-line retained-FFI response-pair seam in both de Bruijn kernels (half a day, dies at phase 8), raising law 5 to 366/96. Recommendation: only if the owner wants row 4 cross-engine before phase 8; otherwise skip.\n- **Q3 (information, no decision needed unless the owner disagrees).** Closures and continuations in the task-wide free environment are refused on the native lanes (R3), admitted everywhere else. If any real composition seeds a de Bruijn kernel's free environment with a closure, say so and R3 becomes a design question instead of a refusal.\n\nREADY\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 +07\n# Slice 6c fix round 1 (engineer report, opus)\n\nBranch `srvm-phase6c`, worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6c`. HEAD is still 47ddb1d9. **Not committed.**\n\nThis round implements work-list items 1 to 7 of `collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.report.md`, in order.\n\n## Scope\n\nI did not edit any design doc, kernel, `rows.cljc`, `semantic_register.cljc` or `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/*`. `git diff --quiet -- docs src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc` is clean.\n\n| File | +/- |\n|---|---|\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc` | +~80 / -7 |\n| `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout_test.cljc` | +~145 / -10 |\n| `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` | +~360 / -70 |\n| `test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj` | +13 / -1 |\n\n## Item 1: F6 and the store-write audit\n\n**Change.**\n- Removed the unused `[yin.vm.debruijn.register :as rvm]` require from `layout_test.cljc`.\n- Added `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc {[\"canonical-task\" \"assoc\"] 1, [\"kernel-task\" \"assoc\"] 1}` to the audit allowlist. The reason written beside it is the ruling's: state construction over keys that already passed the one program-write gate, with the `restore-task` and `isolated-receiver` precedent. These writes are not routed through `store-put`.\n\n**Acceptance.**\n- `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.store-write-audit-test`: 3 tests, 129 assertions, 0 failures.\n- Kondo: `clj -M:kondo --lint` on all four touched files gives **0 errors, 0 warnings**.\n  - `clojure -M:kondo` was refused by this session's permission gate.\n  - `clj -M:kondo` (the same alias) ran.\n\n## Item 2: R3, free-environment values\n\n**Change (`layout.cljc`).**\n- **Lower:** `lower-defect` has a new clause that applies to both de Bruijn kinds. It fires on a `:closure` or `:reified` row in the carried `free-env` slot (slot 5), or one reached through guest collections in it. It refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :free-env-value {:yin.k/path [:free-env sym …]}`. The helpers are `row-procedure` and `free-env-procedure`.\n- **Lift:** `canonical-task` refuses `:yin.k/non-portable :free-env-value {:yin.k/key sym}` when the kernel's `:free-env` is or holds a closure or continuation (`holds-procedure?`). The check runs right after the `:installs` check, so before `rows/lift-task`, which is the only server.\n- Primitives, data and cursor references are still admitted and copied as before.\n\n**Test: `layout-test/free-env-values`.** The fixture is codex's probe 2 (`free-closure-world`): an identity closure `f` made on the world's own lane, placed in `:free-env`, and the program `(then (next-of c) (f \"B\"))`.\n- On the reference lane, both the reference run and `handed-off` answer `\"B\"`. The carrier still admits the value.\n- `lane-lower` into `:stack` and `:register` refuses `[:yin.k/unsupported :free-env-value]` with path `[:free-env f]` and zero attaches.\n- A world built on `:stack` or `:register` refuses at lift with `:non-portable :free-env-value`, key `f`, and zero serves.\n\n**Fails before the fix.** Against HEAD's `layout.cljc` the test gives 10 failures out of 21 assertions.\n\n## Item 3: R1, collision preflight and harness scoping\n\n**Change (`layout.cljc`).** `lower-defect` now opens with the `image-collision` clause. It derives the receiver kind's image identity for every carried A: `:stack` uses `dcode/image-hash` of `derive/stack-image`, `:register` uses `rcode/register-hash` of `derive/register-image`. When two A share one identity it refuses:\n\n```\n:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident :yin.k/segments #{A …}}\n```\n\nThe lift side is unchanged: `canonical-task` raises `:non-portable :ambiguous-image` through `image-segment` before `rows/lift-task` serves anything.\n\n**Change (harness).**\n- New dynamic var `*projected*` and helper `scoped`.\n- `project` also records into `*projected*` when it is bound.\n- `kernel-space` reads `*projected*` when it is bound, and the global `code-index` otherwise.\n- `lane-root` (law 4) and `crossed` (law 5) now run inside `scoped`.\n\n**Test: `layout-test/alpha-equivalent-segments`.**\n- `(fn [x] x)` and `(fn [y] y)` have distinct `:address` and equal `:register-id` and `:stack-id`.\n- **Lift refusal.** A reader parked inside `((fn [x] (then :alpha (next-of c))) 0)`, built on each native lane, is lifted with `layout/space` of the x and y vectors. It refuses `:non-portable :ambiguous-image` with zero serves.\n- **Harness scoping, on both lanes.** With the file-wide index, `lane-lift` also refuses. Inside `scoped`, `kernel-space` holds only the x vector and the lift succeeds.\n- **Lower refusal.** A reference-lane task with both alpha-equivalent closures as store values is lowered into each native lane. It refuses `:unsupported :ambiguous-image`, names both A in `:yin.k/segments`, and makes zero attaches.\n- **Survivor.** The same task lowered into the reference lane passes `h/laws` (laws 1, 2, 3 and 6) and answers `\"B\"`.\n- The fixtures use test-unique shapes (a `:alpha` literal). That way the globally recorded y vector cannot widen another test's unscoped `kernel-space`, for example layout-test's `:closure-value` `(fn [x] x)`.\n\n**Fails before the fix.** I applied two mutants together: the collision clause disabled, and `kernel-space` ignoring `*projected*`. The test then gives 8 failures and 2 errors. The scoping assertions failed (line 492) and the lower refusal assertions failed (lines 504-506).\n\n## Item 4: R4, law-4 controls\n\n**Change (harness).**\n- **Injectivity.** `logical-names` and `ring-names` assert it through `injective`.\n- **`task-root`** is the old `logical-root` body over a decoded task. It also asserts that no lane identity survives in the canonical rows: neither the served ids nor the ring identities.\n- **`lane-root`** now returns `{:root :reference :lane :descriptors :task :machine :peer}`.\n- **`canonical-equality`** asserts:\n  - `(= 3 (count roots))`;\n  - `(= [:semantic-register :stack :register] (mapv :lane roots))`;\n  - one root;\n  - `descriptors-equivalent?` over `logical-descriptors`, which is each stream's `stream/descriptor` with `:dao.stream/identity` renamed under the correspondence.\n- **`logical-positions`** is unchanged apart from its docstring (accepted as is).\n\n**Test: `law-4-controls`** (quick tier, row 1 bare). It runs `canonical-equality`, so the lane, injectivity and descriptor assertions apply, and then checks:\n1. the three lanes' roots are equal;\n2. a cursor position's `:dao.stream.ringbuffer/position` + 1 on the stack lane changes the root;\n3. `:review/extra :different` added to one lane's descriptor fails `descriptors-equivalent?`;\n4. a saved window value changed on the register lane changes the root;\n5. the lexical `a` changed on the reference lane changes the root.\n\nEach control first asserts that it changed the task.\n\n**Mutant reruns** (quick tier, layout-test + harness, 34 tests):\n\n| Mutant | Result |\n|---|---|\n| M11, `(take 1 lanes)` | **Killed**: 37 failures, 3 errors (count and three-lanes assertions in every bare case and in the controls) |\n| M12, every renamed position replaced by `:constant` | **Killed**: 1 failure, the position-offset control |\n| Probe 4, `:review/extra :different` in the register lane's real `logical-descriptors` | **Killed**: 18 failures, the descriptor-equivalence assertion in all 16 bare cases and in the controls |\n\nThe slow tier was not rerun under these mutants, because the quick tier already kills each one.\n\n## Item 5: R5, ambiguous upward tamper\n\n**Change.**\n- No production change: `virt` already refuses ambiguity. This item adds its test.\n- Corpus C moved into the harness as `layout-corpus`; layout-test's `C` now refers to it. The harness requires `yin.vm.parity-test`, `semantic-register.corpus` and `.analysis` for this.\n- New harness helpers:\n  - `ambiguous-dead-slots` finds every `[v p s]` in C where `p` is a reachable non-tail parking pc and `s` is a physical slot of p's body that R gives two or more virtuals, outside `phys(live-out*(p) ∪ {rd})`;\n  - `register-entry` builds a register wait entry for such a pc, with an optional extra slot.\n\n**Test.** `layout-refusals` gains \"a register entry tampered with an ambiguous dead preimage\". It asserts that C holds at least one fixture; it holds **38**. For each fixture:\n- the untampered entry lifts (control);\n- the entry with `s` added refuses `:yin.k/non-portable :register-layout` with `{:yin.k/pc p :yin.k/register s}`.\n\nThe existing `:window-missing` and `:window-extra` cases are unchanged, so all three kinds are pinned.\n\n**Mutant rerun.** M9 makes `virt` return `(first live)` / `(first named)` on ambiguity. It is **killed** with 76 failures (38 × 2). Every fixture exercises the named branch (no live preimage). A mutant that changes only the `(first live)` branch is equivalent on valid segments: `register-pairing` asserts that distinct live virtuals hold distinct slots.\n\n## Item 6: D4 fixtures\n\n**Change (harness).** The primitive refusal in `layout-refusals` is now a table of four cases. Each refuses `[:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers]` on `:register` with zero attaches, and the stack lane continues each one to the reference value.\n\n| Case | Program | Path |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:window` (existing) | `(conj [] (next-of c))` | `[:frames 0 :window r]` |\n| `:lexical` | `((fn [q] (cat (cat [] read) (q [] 1))) conj)` | exactly `[:frames 0 :env q]` |\n| `:captured` | `(let [g (fn [h] (fn [x] (h [] x)))] (let [k (g conj)] (cat (cat [] read) (k 2))))` | exactly `[:frames 0 :env k h]` |\n| `:nested` | `((fn [w r] r) (cat [] conj) read)` | `[:frames 0 :window r 0]` (window register, then vector index 0) |\n\nThe lexical and captured programs read under `cat` rather than `s/then`. `then` and `let1` are lambda applications, so the operator closure saved in the window captures the binding too, and the path would then be `[:frames 0 :window 1 q]`. That is a legitimate refusal, but not the path these fixtures are meant to pin.\n\n**No production fix here, so the mutants show the tests bite:**\n- Mutant A (`value-prim` no longer descends into collections or closures): 6 failures, in the `:captured` and `:nested` cases.\n- Mutant B (`frame-prim` skips lexical bindings): 6 failures, in the `:lexical` and `:captured` cases.\n\n## Item 7: counts\n\nDocstrings and comments now cite §5.3's shared fragment, with the 6c-post-D2 numbers where they apply. The targets are `link-rows`, `unlowerable`, `lane-cases` and the four matrix deftests: law 4 at 16 and 61, law 5 at 92 and 350, rising to 96 and 366 once `unlowerable` is emptied. The asserted counts are unchanged.\n\n## A guard added after item 3\n\n`image-collision` derives images from carried vectors before `rows/lower-task` has validated them. A tampered vector could therefore make `derive` throw a non-`:yin.k` error ahead of the carrier's own first-defect refusal. A vector that fails to derive now gets the unique identity `[::underivable A]`, which leaves its refusal to the carrier.\n- The four-namespace quick tier and the wide quick sweep are both green after this change.\n- No test exercises the guard directly.\n\n## Verification (JVM only, all foreground except where noted)\n\n| Run | Tests | Assertions | Failures | Errors |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n …layout-test -n …safepoint-harness-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -n yin.vm.store-write-audit-test` | 66 | 4274 | 0 | 0 |\n| `-i :slow`, same four namespaces | 4 | 3517 | 0 | 0 |\n| `clojure -M:test -e :slow -r 'yin\\.vm\\.(ucf\\|semantic-register\\|debruijn)\\..*'`, run before and again after the guard | 950 | 22810 | 0 | 0 |\n| `-i :slow`, per namespace (see below) | | | | |\n\n- Kondo: `clj -M:kondo --lint` on all four files gives 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n- Mutants: every source or test mutant was applied over a copy in `collab/p6c-fix1-bak/`. After each one I restored the file and confirmed it with `diff -q` against the copy. `/tmp` copies were refused by the permission gate. `git diff --quiet` on the forbidden paths is clean, and the backup directory has been deleted.\n- Node and Dart were not run, per the brief.\n\n**The wide slow tier.** One `-i :slow -r` run over all three families hit the 20-minute background limit and was stopped, and so did a semantic-register-only run. I then ran the slow tier one namespace at a time. These are all of the namespaces under `ucf`, `semantic-register` and `debruijn` that carry `^:slow`; `debruijn` has none.\n\n| Namespace (slow tier) | Tests | Assertions | Failures | Errors |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test`, `.layout-test`, `ucf.rows-test` | in the four-namespace run above | | 0 | 0 |\n| `semantic-register.derive-test` | 1 | 90 | 0 | 0 |\n| `ucf.safepoint-harness-test`, `ucf.compose-rows-test`, `ucf.holder.driver-test` | 4 | 2286 | 0 | 0 |\n| `ucf.stage-e-rows-test` | 8 | 430 | 0 | 0 |\n| `semantic-register.scan-test` | 3 | 208 | **2** (`scanners-agree-on-the-c4-modules` :205, `goldens-match` :321) | 0 |\n| `ucf.stage-e-process-test` | 13 | 667 | 0 | **13** |\n\n- **scan-test** requires none of the files this round touched. It reads the scanners, the goldens and the yang.python C4 modules. These failures are therefore independent of this diff. I did not rerun them at HEAD to prove they predate it.\n- **stage-e-process-test** errors are environmental. Its Node peers die with `Cannot find module '@noble/hashes/blake3.js'`, because this worktree has no `node_modules`. The brief is JVM only, so I did not run `npm ci`.\n\n## Portability\n\n- **No `for`:** the new code uses `mapcat`, `keep` and `filter`.\n- **Multi-key maps** start from `{}` (`assoc {} …`) or are literals.\n- **No private var access** from tests: `parking-site?` is private to the harness and used only inside it.\n- **No unary minus.**\n- **The new catch** in `image-collision` is `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)`, with `:cljd` first.\n- **The new `binding`** (`*projected*` in `scoped`) wraps only synchronous construction and lift, the same pattern as `*lane*`.\n- **`satisfies?`** use is unchanged.\n\n## Uncertain\n\n- **Item 5 harness search.** `ambiguous-dead-slots` counts only parking pcs (rule A1), not every boundary. That is enough for `lift-state`'s safepoint check, and C yields 38 fixtures.\n- **Global code-index.** Several layout-test helpers still call `h/kernel-space` unscoped: `lifted-waits`, `envs`, `stack-frames` and `closures-round-trip`. They depend on no test projecting an alpha-equivalent of their vectors into the global code-index. I kept item 3's fixtures shape-unique for that reason. Scoping these call sites as well would make the guarantee structural rather than by convention.\n- **Wider coverage.** `free-env-procedure` and `holds-procedure?` also refuse a closure nested in a guest collection in the free environment. The ruling names a closure or continuation \"in\" the free environment; I read nesting as included, because `walk-value` would carry it raw as well.\n---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\necho \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nrun_shard() {\n  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\nprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nrun_ns() {\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\necho \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nxargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\necho \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\nand `git`.\n\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\n\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n\n```sh\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n```\n\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"  # shiva only\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\n  The assertion \"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\" gets no `bind-failed`\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc      |  79 ++++-\n test/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout_test.cljc     | 145 ++++++++-\n .../semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc  | 361 +++++++++++++++++----\n test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj             |  14 +-\n 4 files changed, 517 insertions(+), 82 deletions(-)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.report.md\ncat collab/1792370000000-engineer-6c-fix1.report.md\ngit diff 47ddb1d9..HEAD -- src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.report.md\ncat collab/1792370000000-engineer-6c-fix1.report.md\ngit diff 47ddb1d9..HEAD -- src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj'","aggregated_output":"Created-Local: 2026-10-13 +07\n# Architect rulings for slice 6c (claude-fable-5-1)\n\nRead: codex's review (F1-F6, probes, D1-D5), opus's report, the continuation design §§0.1, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8.3, 11, `layout.cljc` (HEAD 47ddb1d9), the harness lanes, the frame validator's E2, the carrier's `restore-task`, both de Bruijn kernels' `:request-sent` arms and `attach-image`, the semantic-register kernel's `admit-image` and `register-restore`, and `store_write_audit_test.clj`. I ran the audit test once (it fails on exactly the two layout sites the brief names). No doc, source or test was edited.\n\n## Summary (12 lines)\n\n1. Merge 6c after a fix slice of about 2.5 days on opus; no kernel is edited, no acceptance item is dropped silently.\n2. R1: alpha-equivalent segments collide only in the de Bruijn kernels, whose `attach-image` dedupes by R/H; the semantic-register kernel keys code by A and has no collision. Refuse the collision before attach, document it as a phase-8-bounded limit, and test that the survivor is unaffected.\n3. R2: make the 61/350 native matrix normative in §5.3 and §8.3; the reference lane keeps all 89/534. This changes acceptance counts, so it is an OWNER decision (Q1). The retained-FFI seam is cheap (half a day, both kernels) if the owner wants row 4 anyway (Q2); link and install on native lanes are not worth 2-3 days of code that dies at phase 8.\n4. R3: a closure or continuation in the task-wide free environment has no finite canonical form on a de Bruijn kernel (its env would contain itself through the shared free environment); refuse it before attach in both directions on the native lanes only. The carrier and the reference lane keep admitting it.\n5. R4: `canonical-under` may erase a stream marker's identity and descriptor; the harness must then prove descriptor equivalence separately, pin three distinct kernel kinds, assert an injective correspondence, and run four negative controls. `logical-positions` is accepted as is.\n6. R5: adopt codex's D3 wording; an ambiguous upward tamper may be refused `:yin.k/non-portable :register-layout` (amend §8.3 item 12).\n7. R6: D1, D3, D4, D5 accepted as codex wrote them; D2 accepted as a seam specification but moved out of 6c's gate (owner Q2); F2's identity amendment accepted with the provenance-seam sentence replaced (text below).\n8. Store audit: allowlist the two sites with the same reason `rows/restore-task` has; do not route them through `store-put`.\n9. What we give up: native-lane coverage of link, install and retained FFI, and closures in the free environment on native lanes, all until phase 8 deletes those kernels.\n10. What we keep: every invariant (A the only identity, tree always available, sharing survives transport, BLAKE3, no kernel edits in phase 6).\n11. Cost: about 2.5 days opus plus a mechanical doc pass and the three-host run; the optional D2 seam adds half a day as its own slice.\n12. READY once the work list is green on three hosts, kondo clean, audit green, and the owner has answered Q1 (Q2 can wait).\n\n## R1 (F2): `:ambiguous-image`\n\n**Facts.** Both de Bruijn `attach-image`s refuse a second attach whose identity is already a row (`register.cljc:211`, the stack kernel likewise). So a native kernel holding `(fn [x] x)` and `(fn [y] y)` physically holds one image under one R or H; its `:images` table cannot name which A an activation came from, and nothing the layout reads can recover it. The semantic-register kernel's `admit-image` keys code by `jing/segment-key v`, which is A (`semantic_register.cljc:297-300, 431`), so two alpha-equivalent vectors are two code entries and the survivor has no collision at all.\n\n**Ruling: (a).** Option (b) is an edit to both kernels' offset tables and both restores, forbidden in phase 6 and deleted at phase 8; not worth a slice. Refuse the collision as early as possible and document it as a limit bounded by phase 8:\n\n- **Lower** into a de Bruijn kernel: `lower-defect` gains a first clause: when two carried A derive one image of the receiver's kind (`:register-id` or `:stack-id` over the carried `code` slot), refuse `:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident :yin.k/segments #{A …}}` before any attach. Status `:unsupported` because it is the receiver's domain, like `:continuation-registers`; the task is a valid carrier.\n- **Lift** from a de Bruijn kernel: the existing `:yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image` stays, but `canonical-task` must raise it before `lift-context` serves anything (it already runs before `rows/lift-task`, which is the only server; assert zero serves in the test).\n- **Never choose an A.** Agreed with codex.\n- **Harness defect to fix alongside:** `kernel-space` filters the global `code-index` by image checksum, so an alpha-equivalent A from an unrelated fixture widens the refusal. Scope it: `project` records into a `binding`-scoped atom `*projected*` when bound, and `kernel-space` uses that set when bound, the global index otherwise. The space is then the vectors this world projected, never the file's history.\n\n**Wording, §4.1 (replacing codex's \"Additional necessary identity amendment\"):**\n\n> A is the code identity even when distinct A derive identical H or R. Native-image hashes remain checksums and must not select a source segment. The de Bruijn kernels' offset tables carry only the image checksum and refuse a second attach of a known checksum, so a de Bruijn kernel cannot hold two segments of one image. Until phase 8 removes those kernels, a de Bruijn receiver refuses a carried task whose code holds two A of one image before any stream is attached (`:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image`), and a de Bruijn source whose space resolves one image to several A refuses the lift (`:yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image`); no enumeration order selects an A. The semantic-register kernel keys code by A and holds alpha-equivalent segments side by side; this limit is the native kernels', not the carrier's.\n\n**Tests.** `layout-test/alpha-equivalent-segments`: project `(fn [x] x)` and `(fn [y] y)`; assert distinct `:address`, equal `:register-id` and equal `:stack-id`; `layout/space` of both, then `image-segment` through `lift-state` refuses `:ambiguous-image` on both lanes with zero serves; lowering a reference-lane task carrying both closures as store values into each native lane refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image` with zero attaches; the same task lowers into the reference lane, relifts and continues (laws 1 and 2 through `handed-off`), which is the survivor check.\n\nCost: 0.5 day.\n\n## R2 (F3): the 89/534 matrix on the native lanes\n\n**Facts.** Link rows need the harness to answer a `require` with the kernel's own format (H or R) while module responses carry A; install children are whole kernels the layout would have to lift and lower recursively, with a link receiver answering natively; the retained-FFI re-park in both kernels names the composition's own pair (`register.cljc:508-514`, `stack.cljc:564-570`), while the semantic-register kernel honours the carried pair in four lines (`semantic_register.cljc:105-111`).\n\n**Ruling: (a), with the counts made normative; the D2 seam offered separately.** Cost of (b) for link and install is 2-3 days of harness and layout code for kernels deleted at phase 8; I do not recommend it. The D2 seam itself is half a day for both kernels (the four-line `cond->` the semantic-register kernel already has, plus a partial-pair refusal in `lower-defect` rather than in the kernel) but it is a kernel edit, so it needs its own slice (6c-post-D2) and an owner yes. This reduces the acceptance counts of §8.3 items 10 and 11 on the native lanes, so **the owner decides (Q1)**; my recommendation is to accept (a).\n\n**Exact counts under (a).** Law 4: 61 cases (15 wrapped rows × 4 segments + row 20), every case comparing exactly three lanes; the reference lane's law 1/2 matrix stays 89. Law 5: 350 ordered transfers (61 × 6 − 4 × 4 for row 4's four transfers into a de Bruijn receiver); if 6c-post-D2 lands, 366 (61 × 6). Bare tier: law 4 16 cases, law 5 92 (96 with the seam). Tail acceptance (§5.6 item 8): rows 6 and 20 on the native lanes; rows 9 and 12 on the reference lane only.\n\n**Wording, §5.3, append:**\n\n> The de Bruijn lanes execute the shared fragment of the matrix: rows 1-6, 13-16, 18-19, 21-22 and 24 under the four wrappers, and row 20. Rows 7-12 and 17 are not built on them: a de Bruijn kernel links a native image while module responses carry A, and §4 defines no layout for install children. Row 4 is lifted from a de Bruijn source but not lowered into a de Bruijn receiver until the retained-FFI seam (§4, 6c-post-D2) lands: the kernel's re-park names its own call pair. These lanes are a cross-engine check of the canonical frame and are removed with their kernels at phase 8; the reference lane runs the whole matrix.\n\n**Wording, §8.3 items 10 and 11, replace:**\n\n> 10. `harness/cross-engine-canonical-equality`: law 4 for every case of the shared fragment under `canonical-under`: 61 cases, each over exactly three distinct kernel lanes.\n> 11. `harness/cross-engine-lowering`: law 5, six ordered pairs over the shared fragment: 350 transfers (366 once 6c-post-D2 lands, when `unlowerable` is emptied).\n\n**Wording, §4 (new paragraph after the stack tail-completion seam), codex's D2 text accepted verbatim except the last sentence, which becomes:**\n\n> This seam is slice 6c-post-D2, a kernel edit of its own, gated by the owner; it is not a prerequisite of 6c. When it lands, `unlowerable` is emptied and the law-5 counts rise to 366 and 96.\n\n## R3 (F1): free-environment values on the native lanes\n\n**Facts.** `kernel-task` copies `:free-env` raw; `canonical-task` and `lift-context` use the kernel's raw `:free-env`. Codex's probe 2 reproduces the break. Beyond that, the lift direction has no finite answer for a closure *in* the free environment: a de Bruijn closure resolves free names dynamically through the one shared `free-env`, so its canonical `env` is `(merge FE* frames)` where `FE*` contains the closure itself; the canonical closure is cyclic. The semantic-register kernel snapshots `E` at creation, so its closures are finite. E2 checks frame environments only (`frame.cljc:137-143`), never a closure value's `env`, so the carrier admits a closure in the free environment whose env disagrees with the free environment on free names, which a de Bruijn kernel cannot honour.\n\n**Ruling: refuse, do not convert.** Conversion on lower would be sound only under an E2-like agreement condition the validator does not check, and conversion on lift is undefined. The honest and cheapest rule, with a shelf life to phase 8:\n\n- `lower-defect` (both de Bruijn kinds): a `:closure` or `:reified` row value in the carried `free-env` slot refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :free-env-value {:yin.k/path [:free-env sym]}` before any attach. Primitives, data and cursor references stay admitted.\n- `canonical-task` (both de Bruijn kinds): a closure or continuation value in the kernel's `:free-env` refuses `:yin.k/non-portable :free-env-value {:yin.k/key sym}` before anything is served.\n- Everything else in the free environment is copied as now; the values that remain need no conversion (`walk-value` converts only closures and continuations).\n\n**Wording, §6.3, append:**\n\n> A de Bruijn kernel resolves free names through its single `free-env` while a canonical closure snapshots its `env`; a closure or continuation held in the free environment therefore has no finite canonical form on a de Bruijn kernel. Until phase 8, a de Bruijn receiver refuses such a value in the carried `free-env` before attach (`:yin.k/unsupported :free-env-value`, with the path), and a de Bruijn source refuses it at lift before serving. The carrier and the semantic-register kernel admit it.\n\n**Test.** `layout-test/free-env-values`: codex's probe 2 as a fixture (reference-lane identity closure `f` in the initial env, `(then (next-of c) (f \"B\"))`): reference run answers `\"B\"`; `handed-off` on the reference lane answers `\"B\"` (carrier still admits it); `lane-lower` into `:stack` and `:register` refuses `[:yin.k/unsupported :free-env-value]` with zero attaches and the path `[:free-env f]`. Lift side: a native kernel whose `:free-env` holds a `lane-closure` refuses at `canonical-task` with zero serves, on both lanes.\n\nCost: 0.5 day.\n\n## R4 (F4): law 4 negative controls and what `canonical-under` erases\n\n**Ruling on D1: accept codex's §5.5 replacement verbatim.** `canonical-under` may erase exactly one thing: the stream marker's identity and descriptor, replaced by the logical name (`rows.cljc:971-974`). That erasure is design-sanctioned and stays; what it cannot prove, descriptor equivalence, the harness proves separately. `logical-positions` is accepted as written: it rewrites only `:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity` inside a cursor-cell body and keeps every other field, which is exactly the \"replaces only identity-bearing fields, preserves every nonidentity field including offsets\" rule.\n\n**Controls the harness must add (one deftest, quick tier, on row 1 bare):**\n\n1. **Three distinct lanes.** `canonical-equality` asserts `(= [:semantic-register :stack :register] (mapv (comp lane-of :machine) worlds))` for every case, and `(= 3 (count roots))`. Kills M11.\n2. **Injective correspondence.** `logical-names` and `ring-names` assert `(= (count m) (count (distinct (vals m))))` and that `logical-positions` renamed every ringbuffer identity (none of the lane identities survives in the emitted rows).\n3. **Descriptor equivalence.** For each logical name, the three lanes' `stream/descriptor` with identity fields renamed under the correspondence are equal; a descriptor given `:review/extra :different` on one lane fails this assertion (codex's probe 4).\n4. **Position control.** A cursor position's offset changed by one on one lane changes the compared root (`logical-root`). Kills M12.\n5. **Value controls.** A saved window value and a lexical `E` value changed on one lane each change the root.\n\nCost: 0.5 day. No doc change beyond D1.\n\n## R5 (F5, D3): the unique-preimage rule for `virt`\n\n**Ruling: adopt codex's D3 wording verbatim** as an appendix to §4.2 (the live-out* rule, then the sole-named-preimage fallback only for validation, refusal on zero or several, no enumeration order). The fallback is a diagnostic extension, not a layout claim, and lives only until phase 8.\n\n**§8.3 item 12, replace the second clause with:**\n\n> a register entry whose `live` was tampered reconstructs a frame the validator refuses (`:window-extra` for a unique dead slot, `:window-missing` for a dropped live slot), or is refused by the layout itself as `:yin.k/non-portable :register-layout` when the added slot is an ambiguous dead preimage; the test pins all three.\n\n**Test.** Extend `layout-refusals`: find, over C, a body and a reachable non-tail boundary whose `:phys` map sends two or more virtuals to one slot outside `live-out*` and the destination; tamper `:live`/`:regs` with that slot; `lift-state` refuses `:register-layout` (status `:yin.k/non-portable`, the pc and slot in the data). Assert at least one such fixture exists in C (the allocator reuses slots, so it will). Kills M9.\n\nCost: 0.25 day.\n\n## R6: D1-D5 wording, and the store-write audit\n\n- **D1**: accept codex's text verbatim (§5.5). Controls per R4.\n- **D2**: accept the seam text verbatim as the §4 seam specification; last sentence replaced per R2. Not a 6c gate.\n- **D3**: accept verbatim (§4.2 appendix). §8.3 item 12 per R5.\n- **D4**: accept verbatim (§8.3 item 6 rephrased; §4.2/§7.2 paragraph). Add the three refusal fixtures codex asks for: a primitive in a lexical binding, in a closure's captured frame, and nested in a guest collection inside a window; each refuses `:continuation-registers` with the stated path before attach. 0.25 day.\n- **D5**: accept verbatim (§5.2 row 21). No code.\n- **F2 identity amendment**: codex's text with the provenance-seam sentences replaced by my R1 wording above.\n- **F6**: remove the unused `rvm` require in `layout_test.cljc`.\n\n**Store-write audit.** The two sites, `[\"canonical-task\" \"assoc\"] 1` and `[\"kernel-task\" \"assoc\"] 1`, go on the **allowlist**, not through `store-put`. Reason, to write beside the entry: both are state construction over keys that already passed the one program-write gate. `canonical-task` copies a quiescent kernel's store whose every program write went through `engine/put-active` and so `store-put`; `kernel-task` copies the scratch machine's store that `rows/restore-task` built key by key through `store-put` (`rows.cljc:1803`). The layout converts values only (`conv`) and never introduces a key, so a second key check would prove nothing, and `store-put` is per key, so routing through it would mean a reduce that re-checks what the carrier's step already checked. This is precisely the precedent of `rows.cljc` `[\"restore-task\" \"assoc\"]` and `handoff.cljc` `[\"isolated-receiver\" \"assoc\"]`.\n\n## Work list for opus (priority order, with acceptance)\n\n1. **F6 and the audit** (0.2 d). Drop the unused require; add the two allowlist entries with the reason above. Accept: `clj -M:kondo` 0 warnings on the three files; `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.store-write-audit-test` green.\n2. **R3 free-env refusals** (0.5 d). `lower-defect` and `canonical-task` as ruled. Accept: `layout-test/free-env-values` as specified, zero attaches and zero serves asserted.\n3. **R1 collision preflight and harness scoping** (0.5 d). `lower-defect` first clause; `*projected*` scoping of `kernel-space`. Accept: `layout-test/alpha-equivalent-segments` as specified, including the reference-lane survivor round trip.\n4. **R4 law-4 controls** (0.5 d). The five controls in one deftest; the three-lane and injectivity assertions inside `canonical-equality` and the name maps. Accept: M11 and M12 re-applied are killed; codex's probe 4 descriptor change fails the descriptor assertion.\n5. **R5 ambiguous preimage** (0.25 d). Accept: M9 re-applied is killed; `layout-refusals` pins `:window-extra`, `:window-missing` and `:register-layout`.\n6. **D4 fixtures** (0.25 d). Accept: three new refusals with paths, zero attaches.\n7. **Counts** (0.1 d). Docstrings of `link-rows`, `unlowerable` and the two matrices cite §5.3's new paragraph; counts unchanged (61/350, 16/92) unless Q2 lands first.\n8. **Doc pass** (0.25 d, mechanical, may be a separate claude -p): apply the verbatim texts in R1, R2, R3, R4 (D1), R5 (D3 and item 12), R6 (D2, D4, D5, F2) by section; §11 gains Q1 and Q2 and their answers.\n9. **Three hosts** (acceptance 13). `bb test:sub yin.vm` on JVM, Node, Dart in the foreground; `satisfies?`, `binding` and `memoize` are pinned-source-supported on Dart per codex but unrun. Accept: three green lanes reported with counts.\n\nTotal about 2.5 days plus the three-host run. Re-review by codex on the diff of items 2-6 only.\n\n## Open questions for the OWNER\n\n- **Q1 (scope, changes acceptance counts).** Accept the native-lane matrix as the shared fragment: law 4 61 of 89, law 5 350 of 534, tail acceptance rows 6 and 20 only, with the reason that those kernels die at phase 8 and the reference lane keeps the full matrix. Recommendation: yes.\n- **Q2 (kernel edit, own slice).** Land 6c-post-D2, the four-line retained-FFI response-pair seam in both de Bruijn kernels (half a day, dies at phase 8), raising law 5 to 366/96. Recommendation: only if the owner wants row 4 cross-engine before phase 8; otherwise skip.\n- **Q3 (information, no decision needed unless the owner disagrees).** Closures and continuations in the task-wide free environment are refused on the native lanes (R3), admitted everywhere else. If any real composition seeds a de Bruijn kernel's free environment with a closure, say so and R3 becomes a design question instead of a refusal.\n\nREADY\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 +07\n# Slice 6c fix round 1 (engineer report, opus)\n\nBranch `srvm-phase6c`, worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6c`. HEAD is still 47ddb1d9. **Not committed.**\n\nThis round implements work-list items 1 to 7 of `collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.report.md`, in order.\n\n## Scope\n\nI did not edit any design doc, kernel, `rows.cljc`, `semantic_register.cljc` or `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/*`. `git diff --quiet -- docs src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc` is clean.\n\n| File | +/- |\n|---|---|\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc` | +~80 / -7 |\n| `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout_test.cljc` | +~145 / -10 |\n| `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` | +~360 / -70 |\n| `test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj` | +13 / -1 |\n\n## Item 1: F6 and the store-write audit\n\n**Change.**\n- Removed the unused `[yin.vm.debruijn.register :as rvm]` require from `layout_test.cljc`.\n- Added `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc {[\"canonical-task\" \"assoc\"] 1, [\"kernel-task\" \"assoc\"] 1}` to the audit allowlist. The reason written beside it is the ruling's: state construction over keys that already passed the one program-write gate, with the `restore-task` and `isolated-receiver` precedent. These writes are not routed through `store-put`.\n\n**Acceptance.**\n- `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.store-write-audit-test`: 3 tests, 129 assertions, 0 failures.\n- Kondo: `clj -M:kondo --lint` on all four touched files gives **0 errors, 0 warnings**.\n  - `clojure -M:kondo` was refused by this session's permission gate.\n  - `clj -M:kondo` (the same alias) ran.\n\n## Item 2: R3, free-environment values\n\n**Change (`layout.cljc`).**\n- **Lower:** `lower-defect` has a new clause that applies to both de Bruijn kinds. It fires on a `:closure` or `:reified` row in the carried `free-env` slot (slot 5), or one reached through guest collections in it. It refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :free-env-value {:yin.k/path [:free-env sym …]}`. The helpers are `row-procedure` and `free-env-procedure`.\n- **Lift:** `canonical-task` refuses `:yin.k/non-portable :free-env-value {:yin.k/key sym}` when the kernel's `:free-env` is or holds a closure or continuation (`holds-procedure?`). The check runs right after the `:installs` check, so before `rows/lift-task`, which is the only server.\n- Primitives, data and cursor references are still admitted and copied as before.\n\n**Test: `layout-test/free-env-values`.** The fixture is codex's probe 2 (`free-closure-world`): an identity closure `f` made on the world's own lane, placed in `:free-env`, and the program `(then (next-of c) (f \"B\"))`.\n- On the reference lane, both the reference run and `handed-off` answer `\"B\"`. The carrier still admits the value.\n- `lane-lower` into `:stack` and `:register` refuses `[:yin.k/unsupported :free-env-value]` with path `[:free-env f]` and zero attaches.\n- A world built on `:stack` or `:register` refuses at lift with `:non-portable :free-env-value`, key `f`, and zero serves.\n\n**Fails before the fix.** Against HEAD's `layout.cljc` the test gives 10 failures out of 21 assertions.\n\n## Item 3: R1, collision preflight and harness scoping\n\n**Change (`layout.cljc`).** `lower-defect` now opens with the `image-collision` clause. It derives the receiver kind's image identity for every carried A: `:stack` uses `dcode/image-hash` of `derive/stack-image`, `:register` uses `rcode/register-hash` of `derive/register-image`. When two A share one identity it refuses:\n\n```\n:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident :yin.k/segments #{A …}}\n```\n\nThe lift side is unchanged: `canonical-task` raises `:non-portable :ambiguous-image` through `image-segment` before `rows/lift-task` serves anything.\n\n**Change (harness).**\n- New dynamic var `*projected*` and helper `scoped`.\n- `project` also records into `*projected*` when it is bound.\n- `kernel-space` reads `*projected*` when it is bound, and the global `code-index` otherwise.\n- `lane-root` (law 4) and `crossed` (law 5) now run inside `scoped`.\n\n**Test: `layout-test/alpha-equivalent-segments`.**\n- `(fn [x] x)` and `(fn [y] y)` have distinct `:address` and equal `:register-id` and `:stack-id`.\n- **Lift refusal.** A reader parked inside `((fn [x] (then :alpha (next-of c))) 0)`, built on each native lane, is lifted with `layout/space` of the x and y vectors. It refuses `:non-portable :ambiguous-image` with zero serves.\n- **Harness scoping, on both lanes.** With the file-wide index, `lane-lift` also refuses. Inside `scoped`, `kernel-space` holds only the x vector and the lift succeeds.\n- **Lower refusal.** A reference-lane task with both alpha-equivalent closures as store values is lowered into each native lane. It refuses `:unsupported :ambiguous-image`, names both A in `:yin.k/segments`, and makes zero attaches.\n- **Survivor.** The same task lowered into the reference lane passes `h/laws` (laws 1, 2, 3 and 6) and answers `\"B\"`.\n- The fixtures use test-unique shapes (a `:alpha` literal). That way the globally recorded y vector cannot widen another test's unscoped `kernel-space`, for example layout-test's `:closure-value` `(fn [x] x)`.\n\n**Fails before the fix.** I applied two mutants together: the collision clause disabled, and `kernel-space` ignoring `*projected*`. The test then gives 8 failures and 2 errors. The scoping assertions failed (line 492) and the lower refusal assertions failed (lines 504-506).\n\n## Item 4: R4, law-4 controls\n\n**Change (harness).**\n- **Injectivity.** `logical-names` and `ring-names` assert it through `injective`.\n- **`task-root`** is the old `logical-root` body over a decoded task. It also asserts that no lane identity survives in the canonical rows: neither the served ids nor the ring identities.\n- **`lane-root`** now returns `{:root :reference :lane :descriptors :task :machine :peer}`.\n- **`canonical-equality`** asserts:\n  - `(= 3 (count roots))`;\n  - `(= [:semantic-register :stack :register] (mapv :lane roots))`;\n  - one root;\n  - `descriptors-equivalent?` over `logical-descriptors`, which is each stream's `stream/descriptor` with `:dao.stream/identity` renamed under the correspondence.\n- **`logical-positions`** is unchanged apart from its docstring (accepted as is).\n\n**Test: `law-4-controls`** (quick tier, row 1 bare). It runs `canonical-equality`, so the lane, injectivity and descriptor assertions apply, and then checks:\n1. the three lanes' roots are equal;\n2. a cursor position's `:dao.stream.ringbuffer/position` + 1 on the stack lane changes the root;\n3. `:review/extra :different` added to one lane's descriptor fails `descriptors-equivalent?`;\n4. a saved window value changed on the register lane changes the root;\n5. the lexical `a` changed on the reference lane changes the root.\n\nEach control first asserts that it changed the task.\n\n**Mutant reruns** (quick tier, layout-test + harness, 34 tests):\n\n| Mutant | Result |\n|---|---|\n| M11, `(take 1 lanes)` | **Killed**: 37 failures, 3 errors (count and three-lanes assertions in every bare case and in the controls) |\n| M12, every renamed position replaced by `:constant` | **Killed**: 1 failure, the position-offset control |\n| Probe 4, `:review/extra :different` in the register lane's real `logical-descriptors` | **Killed**: 18 failures, the descriptor-equivalence assertion in all 16 bare cases and in the controls |\n\nThe slow tier was not rerun under these mutants, because the quick tier already kills each one.\n\n## Item 5: R5, ambiguous upward tamper\n\n**Change.**\n- No production change: `virt` already refuses ambiguity. This item adds its test.\n- Corpus C moved into the harness as `layout-corpus`; layout-test's `C` now refers to it. The harness requires `yin.vm.parity-test`, `semantic-register.corpus` and `.analysis` for this.\n- New harness helpers:\n  - `ambiguous-dead-slots` finds every `[v p s]` in C where `p` is a reachable non-tail parking pc and `s` is a physical slot of p's body that R gives two or more virtuals, outside `phys(live-out*(p) ∪ {rd})`;\n  - `register-entry` builds a register wait entry for such a pc, with an optional extra slot.\n\n**Test.** `layout-refusals` gains \"a register entry tampered with an ambiguous dead preimage\". It asserts that C holds at least one fixture; it holds **38**. For each fixture:\n- the untampered entry lifts (control);\n- the entry with `s` added refuses `:yin.k/non-portable :register-layout` with `{:yin.k/pc p :yin.k/register s}`.\n\nThe existing `:window-missing` and `:window-extra` cases are unchanged, so all three kinds are pinned.\n\n**Mutant rerun.** M9 makes `virt` return `(first live)` / `(first named)` on ambiguity. It is **killed** with 76 failures (38 × 2). Every fixture exercises the named branch (no live preimage). A mutant that changes only the `(first live)` branch is equivalent on valid segments: `register-pairing` asserts that distinct live virtuals hold distinct slots.\n\n## Item 6: D4 fixtures\n\n**Change (harness).** The primitive refusal in `layout-refusals` is now a table of four cases. Each refuses `[:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers]` on `:register` with zero attaches, and the stack lane continues each one to the reference value.\n\n| Case | Program | Path |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:window` (existing) | `(conj [] (next-of c))` | `[:frames 0 :window r]` |\n| `:lexical` | `((fn [q] (cat (cat [] read) (q [] 1))) conj)` | exactly `[:frames 0 :env q]` |\n| `:captured` | `(let [g (fn [h] (fn [x] (h [] x)))] (let [k (g conj)] (cat (cat [] read) (k 2))))` | exactly `[:frames 0 :env k h]` |\n| `:nested` | `((fn [w r] r) (cat [] conj) read)` | `[:frames 0 :window r 0]` (window register, then vector index 0) |\n\nThe lexical and captured programs read under `cat` rather than `s/then`. `then` and `let1` are lambda applications, so the operator closure saved in the window captures the binding too, and the path would then be `[:frames 0 :window 1 q]`. That is a legitimate refusal, but not the path these fixtures are meant to pin.\n\n**No production fix here, so the mutants show the tests bite:**\n- Mutant A (`value-prim` no longer descends into collections or closures): 6 failures, in the `:captured` and `:nested` cases.\n- Mutant B (`frame-prim` skips lexical bindings): 6 failures, in the `:lexical` and `:captured` cases.\n\n## Item 7: counts\n\nDocstrings and comments now cite §5.3's shared fragment, with the 6c-post-D2 numbers where they apply. The targets are `link-rows`, `unlowerable`, `lane-cases` and the four matrix deftests: law 4 at 16 and 61, law 5 at 92 and 350, rising to 96 and 366 once `unlowerable` is emptied. The asserted counts are unchanged.\n\n## A guard added after item 3\n\n`image-collision` derives images from carried vectors before `rows/lower-task` has validated them. A tampered vector could therefore make `derive` throw a non-`:yin.k` error ahead of the carrier's own first-defect refusal. A vector that fails to derive now gets the unique identity `[::underivable A]`, which leaves its refusal to the carrier.\n- The four-namespace quick tier and the wide quick sweep are both green after this change.\n- No test exercises the guard directly.\n\n## Verification (JVM only, all foreground except where noted)\n\n| Run | Tests | Assertions | Failures | Errors |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n …layout-test -n …safepoint-harness-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -n yin.vm.store-write-audit-test` | 66 | 4274 | 0 | 0 |\n| `-i :slow`, same four namespaces | 4 | 3517 | 0 | 0 |\n| `clojure -M:test -e :slow -r 'yin\\.vm\\.(ucf\\|semantic-register\\|debruijn)\\..*'`, run before and again after the guard | 950 | 22810 | 0 | 0 |\n| `-i :slow`, per namespace (see below) | | | | |\n\n- Kondo: `clj -M:kondo --lint` on all four files gives 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n- Mutants: every source or test mutant was applied over a copy in `collab/p6c-fix1-bak/`. After each one I restored the file and confirmed it with `diff -q` against the copy. `/tmp` copies were refused by the permission gate. `git diff --quiet` on the forbidden paths is clean, and the backup directory has been deleted.\n- Node and Dart were not run, per the brief.\n\n**The wide slow tier.** One `-i :slow -r` run over all three families hit the 20-minute background limit and was stopped, and so did a semantic-register-only run. I then ran the slow tier one namespace at a time. These are all of the namespaces under `ucf`, `semantic-register` and `debruijn` that carry `^:slow`; `debruijn` has none.\n\n| Namespace (slow tier) | Tests | Assertions | Failures | Errors |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test`, `.layout-test`, `ucf.rows-test` | in the four-namespace run above | | 0 | 0 |\n| `semantic-register.derive-test` | 1 | 90 | 0 | 0 |\n| `ucf.safepoint-harness-test`, `ucf.compose-rows-test`, `ucf.holder.driver-test` | 4 | 2286 | 0 | 0 |\n| `ucf.stage-e-rows-test` | 8 | 430 | 0 | 0 |\n| `semantic-register.scan-test` | 3 | 208 | **2** (`scanners-agree-on-the-c4-modules` :205, `goldens-match` :321) | 0 |\n| `ucf.stage-e-process-test` | 13 | 667 | 0 | **13** |\n\n- **scan-test** requires none of the files this round touched. It reads the scanners, the goldens and the yang.python C4 modules. These failures are therefore independent of this diff. I did not rerun them at HEAD to prove they predate it.\n- **stage-e-process-test** errors are environmental. Its Node peers die with `Cannot find module '@noble/hashes/blake3.js'`, because this worktree has no `node_modules`. The brief is JVM only, so I did not run `npm ci`.\n\n## Portability\n\n- **No `for`:** the new code uses `mapcat`, `keep` and `filter`.\n- **Multi-key maps** start from `{}` (`assoc {} …`) or are literals.\n- **No private var access** from tests: `parking-site?` is private to the harness and used only inside it.\n- **No unary minus.**\n- **The new catch** in `image-collision` is `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)`, with `:cljd` first.\n- **The new `binding`** (`*projected*` in `scoped`) wraps only synchronous construction and lift, the same pattern as `*lane*`.\n- **`satisfies?`** use is unchanged.\n\n## Uncertain\n\n- **Item 5 harness search.** `ambiguous-dead-slots` counts only parking pcs (rule A1), not every boundary. That is enough for `lift-state`'s safepoint check, and C yields 38 fixtures.\n- **Global code-index.** Several layout-test helpers still call `h/kernel-space` unscoped: `lifted-waits`, `envs`, `stack-frames` and `closures-round-trip`. They depend on no test projecting an alpha-equivalent of their vectors into the global code-index. I kept item 3's fixtures shape-unique for that reason. Scoping these call sites as well would make the guarantee structural rather than by convention.\n- **Wider coverage.** `free-env-procedure` and `holds-procedure?` also refuse a closure nested in a guest collection in the free environment. The ruling names a closure or continuation \"in\" the free environment; I read nesting as included, because `walk-value` would carry it raw as well.\ndiff --git a/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc b/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc\nindex d9b4ab77..49e5ef53 100644\n--- a/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc\n+++ b/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc\n@@ -700,18 +700,33 @@\n ;; Tasks\n ;; =============================================================================\n \n+(defn- holds-procedure?\n+  \"Whether kernel value `x` is or holds, through guest collections, a\n+   closure or continuation.\"\n+  [x]\n+  (cond\n+    (or (values/closure? x) (values/continuation? x)) true\n+    (values/host-typed? x) false\n+    (map? x) (boolean (some holds-procedure? (concat (keys x) (vals x))))\n+    (coll? x) (boolean (some holds-procedure? x))\n+    :else false))\n+\n+\n (defn canonical-task\n   \"Quiescent kernel `k` presented as semantic-register machine `template`\n    (a fresh machine of the same composition) for `rows/lift-task`: code\n    by A from `space`, every wait, parked record and value reconstructed\n    through the layout; resources, stores and counters carried. A running\n    kernel is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; install children are not carried\n-   by this phase's layouts.\"\n+   by this phase's layouts; a closure or continuation in the free\n+   environment has no finite canonical form (§6.3).\"\n   [k space template]\n   (when (engine/active-continuation? k)\n     (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc (:pc k)}))\n   (when (seq (:installs k))\n     (refuse! :yin.k/unsupported :installs {:yin.k/names (vec (keys (:installs k)))}))\n+  (when-let [sym (some (fn [[sym x]] (when (holds-procedure? x) sym)) (sort-by (comp str key) (:free-env k)))]\n+    (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :free-env-value {:yin.k/key sym}))\n   (let [cx (lift-context k space (:owner template))\n         conv #(canonical-value cx %)\n         parked (into {} (map (fn [[id rec]] [id (assoc (lift-state cx rec) :type :parked-continuation :id id)]))\n@@ -859,20 +874,70 @@\n       nil)))\n \n \n+(defn- row-procedure\n+  \"The path to the first closure or continuation row carrier value `x`\n+   holds, through guest collections; or nil.\"\n+  [x path]\n+  (when (and (vector? x) (= :row (first x)))\n+    (let [n (second x)]\n+      (case (:kind n)\n+        (:closure :reified) path\n+        (:vec :list :set :map) (some (fn [[i y]] (row-procedure y (conj path i))) (map-indexed vector (:slots n)))\n+        nil))))\n+\n+\n+(defn- free-env-procedure\n+  \"§6.3: a de Bruijn kernel resolves free names through its one\n+   `free-env` while a canonical closure snapshots its `env`, so a closure\n+   or continuation held in the free environment has no finite canonical\n+   form there. The path `[:free-env sym …]` to the first such value of\n+   decoded task `task`, or nil.\"\n+  [task]\n+  (some (fn [[sym x]] (row-procedure x [:free-env sym]))\n+        (partition 2 (:slots (nth (:slots task) 5)))))\n+\n+\n+(defn- image-collision\n+  \"§4.1: `[ident #{A …}]` for the first image of kind `kind` two carried A\n+   derive, or nil. A de Bruijn kernel attaches one image once, so it\n+   cannot hold both segments; no enumeration order selects an A. A\n+   vector that derives no image is left to the carrier's own checks.\"\n+  [code kind]\n+  (let [ident (fn [a v]\n+                (try\n+                  (if (= :stack kind)\n+                    (dcode/image-hash (:image (derive/stack-image v)))\n+                    (rcode/register-hash (derive/register-image v)))\n+                  (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _ [::underivable a])))\n+        by-image (reduce (fn [acc [a v]] (update acc (ident a v) (fnil conj #{}) a)) {} code)]\n+    (some (fn [[i as]] (when (< 1 (count as)) [i as])) (sort-by (comp str key) by-image))))\n+\n+\n (defn lower-defect\n   \"What a kernel of `kind` cannot hold, judged on decoded task `task`\n-   before anything is attached: install children (not carried by this\n-   phase's layouts), and for the register kernel a primitive function in\n-   the payload data of a wait or parked state (a window, a lexical\n-   binding, or reached through them), which its restore refuses as\n-   `:continuation-registers` (`vm/machine-data?`). nil when it can.\"\n+   before anything is attached: two carried A of one image (§4.1), install\n+   children (not carried by this phase's layouts), a closure or\n+   continuation in the free environment (§6.3), and for the register\n+   kernel a primitive function in the payload data of a wait or parked\n+   state (a window, a lexical binding, or reached through them), which\n+   its restore refuses as `:continuation-registers` (`vm/machine-data?`).\n+   nil when it can.\"\n   [task kind]\n   (let [slots (:slots task)\n-        code (nth slots 11)]\n+        code (nth slots 11)\n+        collision (image-collision code kind)\n+        free (free-env-procedure task)]\n     (cond\n+      collision\n+      (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :ambiguous-image\n+             :yin.k/image (first collision) :yin.k/segments (second collision))\n+\n       (seq (nth slots 12))\n       (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :installs)\n \n+      free\n+      (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :free-env-value :yin.k/path free)\n+\n       (= :register kind)\n       (when-let [path (or (some (fn [[i w]] (state-prim code (first (:slots w)) [:frames i]))\n                                 (map-indexed vector (nth slots 13)))\ndiff --git a/test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj b/test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj\nindex d63ae214..85f81f78 100644\n--- a/test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj\n+++ b/test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj\n@@ -379,7 +379,19 @@\n    \"src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc\"\n    {[\"row-table\" :map] 1\n     [\"restore-task\" \"engine/store-put\"] 1\n-    [\"restore-task\" \"assoc\"] 1}})\n+    [\"restore-task\" \"assoc\"] 1}\n+   ;; the de Bruijn layouts' task presentations (continuation design\n+   ;; §7.1, §7.2): state construction over keys that already passed the\n+   ;; one program-write gate. canonical-task copies a quiescent kernel's\n+   ;; store, whose every program write went through engine/put-active and\n+   ;; so store-put; kernel-task copies the scratch machine's store that\n+   ;; rows/restore-task built key by key through store-put. The layout\n+   ;; converts values only and never introduces a key, so a second key\n+   ;; check would prove nothing -- the precedent of restore-task and\n+   ;; isolated-receiver above\n+   \"src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc\"\n+   {[\"canonical-task\" \"assoc\"] 1\n+    [\"kernel-task\" \"assoc\"] 1}})\n \n \n (deftest every-store-mutation-is-on-the-allowlist\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
